ive position between them in  this representation and king and queen  will be somewhat the same so they take  into account this kind of these kind of  um  you know semantic meanings of words  and also synonyms  so two of the well-known  embeddings are word to vec and glove  and  you can just these are available on the  on the web you can download them you  decide how many dimensions you want and  you just plug it into your network  okay  so we used an rnn with a word embedding  on the reviews data and after a lot of  work the results are disappointing 76  accuracy remember we got close to 89  percent accuracy  okay so then we fit a more exotic  recurrent neural network than the one  that we displayed  it's once known as the lstm which stands  for long and short-term memory  and without getting into great detail  yeah  al receives input both from a l minus 1  the previous hidden unit  that's the short-term memory as it well  is from a version that reaches further  back in time so you take big steps back  in time and and get an ale  back from there as well  right so that's the long-term memory  and when you do that it takes much  longer to train you now get 87 accuracy  which is slightly less than 88 percent  achieved by clement it takes a long time  to learn  now just like in the amnes data and in  the resnet and all these data bases  these data these imdb reviews have been  used as a benchmark for new rnn  architectures  so the best reported result found at the  time of writing was around 95  it's of course a much more complex  network  if you look in section 10.5.1 of the  book the second edition you'll see a  leader will point to a leaderboard  where you can see who's winning the race  and again subject to to overfit in and  and over over looking at the data  now this is something we've seen pretty  often right i mean  deep nets have some spectacular  successes but on a lot of problems  they don't do better or even they do  worse than simpler methods yes you don't  hear about that very much because it's  harder to publish simple things right  that's right  so just a reminder to  not only try deep nets and think fancy  methods but also try simpler methods  because they often work as well and  they're much simpler to understand
 

